text:
lisa_sales: Heads-up: ACME mobile demo failed again. Their Android webview client received a truncated JSON item then the SSE stream hung. They reported it happened ~75s into a 2.5k token reply. Can we triage?

demo_bot: (system) client: acme-mob-21, req: req_f9a2, url: /v1/generate/stream, user-agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Linux; Android) WebView/537.36

tom_se: Mobile webviews sometimes aggressively coalesce TCP frames or buffer for power reasons. Do we have the raw bytes or a capture from the device?

lisa_sales: They attached a capture: show below as text (sanitized): ```
data: {"chunk":1,"text":"Start..."}

data: {"chunk":2,"text":"middle...

data: {"chunk":3,"text":"end"}
```

ryan_runtime: I see chunk 2 missing its closing quote/newline sequence. Server side logs show no error; runtime reported chunk flush but socket experienced a FIN_WAIT from the client side. Could be the webview's network stack closing idle sockets when backgrounded.

emma_support: Were they backgrounded? The customer said they switched apps for a minute. Backgrounding can trigger Android to pause network activity for WebView. That explains the ~75s gap.

tom_se: Two mitigation paths: 1) make streaming resilient by emitting lightweight keepalive comments every 10-15s to keep the OS network active; 2) the client should assemble data events across `data:` lines and only JSON.parse once a full object boundary is seen. Also using EventSource polyfill that retries on partial frames helps.

lisa_sales: Can you paste a minimal keepalive we can ask them to add on the server? They are using our hosted endpoint (no proxy on our side).

ryan_runtime: Server-side keepalive example (SSE):
```
// every 12s
res.write(': ka

'); // comment ping keeps connection alive
res.flush();
```
We can also switch to `event: ka
data: {}

` if they want an explicit event.

emma_support: Also recommend they set the EventSource `withCredentials` correctly for auth and avoid XHR fallbacks that may buffer. For Android WebView, using the `fetch()` stream + ReadableStream sometimes is more reliable than EventSource. Example client snippet:```
const r = await fetch('/v1/generate/stream');
const reader = r.body.getReader();
let buf = '';
while(true){const {value,done}=await reader.read(); if(done) break; buf += new TextDecoder().decode(value); processChunks(buf); }
```
This avoids WebView SSE quirks.

tom_se: Note: if the client uses fetch streaming you must ensure CORS allows streaming and the intermediary (CDN) doesn't buffer.

demo_bot: Customer says they route through CloudFront for demos. CloudFront can buffer by default for large responses; set `Cache-Control: no-transform` and `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` plus configure distribution to not compress/transform streaming endpoints.

ryan_runtime: Quick server-side canary: I'll enable 12s keepalive for the demo customer's dedicated slot and add a `X-KA-ENABLED: true` header so we can verify on logs. ETA 15m.

lisa_sales: On the call in 20m. Please post the canary URL when ready. Also ask ACME to test with app foregrounded vs backgrounded.

emma_support: Small client parser to avoid crashes on partial JSON:```
let buffer = '';
source.onmessage = e => {
  buffer += e.data;
  // only try parse when buffer ends with a newline or '}' char
  if(buffer.trim().endsWith('}') || buffer.trim().endsWith("]'") ){
    try{ const obj = JSON.parse(buffer); buffer = ''; handle(obj); }catch(_){ /* wait */ }
  }
};
```
(we noted many mobile clients split multi-byte characters; using TextDecoder in fetch path prevents mid-codepoint splits)

lisa_sales: Thanks. If CloudFront is the blocker we can ask them to temporarily bypass. Any logging we should ask them to capture?

tom_se: Ask for `tcpdump` / `adb logcat` if possible, and show whether the FIN comes from client. Also collect our runtime request id -> we'll correlate via `req_f9a2`.

emma_support: I'll follow up with a short how-to note for Android WebView devs and add it to the POC playbook.

lisa_sales: Great, keep me posted. :rocket:
